## SECTION 4: SERP_ANALYSIS
**competitor_gaps** (demand-validated on 2+ sources):
1. No source gives a clean **side-by-side monthly cost table** — Polish Pops/Nailicy claim gel strips are cheaper long-term (2x cost of press-ons/month) while press-ons have lower upfront price; nobody resolves this into one clear framework.
2. **HEMA allergy safety as a head-to-head comparison point** is validated across 5+ dedicated sources (NailKnowledge, HeyAllergy, Lit Gels, Velvora, NOOVES) but is absent as a section in the direct "gel strips vs press-on" comparison articles — it's treated as its own separate topic, not integrated into the comparison.
3. **"Green nail" / bacterial infection risk from air pockets under press-ons** validated on NOOVES + supported by dermatology-adjacent sources — rarely mentioned outside that one brand's content.

**missing_semantic_territory:** allergy/HEMA-free framing inside a comparison context; clear monthly-cost math; the "why press-ons trap moisture, gel strips don't" structural explanation.

**our_strategic_approach:** Lead with the wear-time gap (validated 10-14+ days vs 3-7 days across 8+ sources) as the hook, then differentiate by resolving the two gaps competitors leave open — an honest cost-per-month breakdown and a clear HEMA/allergy safety comparison — giving readers the decisive, well-rounded answer competitor articles only partially deliver.

**recommended_image_count_validation:** Comparison intent = 5 images per phase_2 rule_4. Confirmed against SERP: competitor pages use hero + before/after or product-application shots, not galleries — 5 images (1 hero + 4 body) matches pattern.

