## SECTION 5: AUDIENCE_INTELLIGENCE
**who_they_are:** Someone already familiar with both product categories (has tried press-ons, possibly disappointed by lifting/popping) who has seen semi-cured gel strips trending on TikTok/Pinterest and wants a clear-eyed comparison before switching or spending money on a UV lamp.

**what_they_want_from_this_post:** A definitive, non-salesy verdict — which one actually lasts longer, costs less over time, and won't irritate sensitive skin — not a brand's marketing page dressed as a comparison.

**what_frustrates_them:** Most comparison articles are published by gel-strip brands themselves (Husnaa, Nous, NOOVES) so the "verdict" always favors gel strips; readers can't tell if the durability claims (14+ days) are realistic for their own hands, and cost comparisons are vague or missing.

**top_5_pain_points:**
1. Confusion over whether "semi-cured gel strips" are just fancy stickers or actually different from press-ons (validated: Quora, Husnaa "cheap sticker myth" section)
2. Fear of nail damage from either product — specifically glue residue from press-ons vs. UV lamp/curing concerns for gel strips
3. Uncertainty about needing to buy a UV/LED lamp just to try gel strips
4. Not knowing which option suits sensitive skin/HEMA allergies
5. Wanting a real cost comparison, not just "which lasts longer" in isolation

**exact_user_phrases:** "cheap sticker myth"; "which lasts longer"; "salon-quality at home"; "no glue needed"; "still be damaged"; "gel-cured press-on nail"; "flush vs bulky"; "green nail syndrome"; "structural anatomical mismatch"; "lifting at the edges"; "one-time use only"; "HEMA-free"

**intent_content_signals:** Comparison tables perform well (used by Husnaa, Nailicy); readers respond to real-life scenario breakdowns (gym, pool, cooking) over lab-condition claims; direct verdict statements expected by the end; numbered pros/cons format favored; FAQ block expected at close.

